\\"Invalid scene index\\\");\\n                        continue;\\n                    }\\n                };\\n\\n                scenes.remove(scene_idx);\\n                println!(\\\"Scene deleted\\\");\\n            }\\n            12 => {\\n                println!(\\\"\\\\nCleaning up and exiting...\\\");\\n                shape_manager_cleanup();\\n                println!(\\\"Goodbye!\\\");\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            _ => {\\n                println!(\\\"Invalid choice\\\");\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Planning the Rust Migration**\n\nI'm currently focused on the initial setup. I'm thinking about the structure of the Rust project now. I'll need a `Cargo.toml` file to manage dependencies and build configuration, and a `src/main.rs` file for the entry point. I'm considering how best to mirror the C project's structure, specifically the `.h` and `.c` files, in Rust modules and files.\n\n\n**Mapping C Code to Rust**\n\nI've been immersed in dissecting the C code, specifically `shape.h` and `shape.c`. My current focus is on translating the `shape_t` struct and `shape_type_t` enum into their Rust equivalents. I'm leaning toward using a Rust `enum` for `ShapeType` and a `struct` with immutable fields for `Shape`. I'm also considering the best way to handle the singleton pattern used for shape management, leaning towards `const` or `static` items or a function returning `&'static Shape` given the immutability of the data. I've also drafted the initial Rust `Shape` struct.\n\n\n**Outlining Scene Implementation**\n\nI'm now shifting my focus to `scene.h` and `scene.c`. I need to figure out how to represent the scene data and associated operations in Rust. I'm considering the best way to handle scene objects, likely through a struct or an enum. I need to understand how the scene interacts with shapes. The C code uses `scene_add_shape` and related functions. I'll need Rust equiv